    ("cpp-cas-counter-retry", "The loser recomputes from the refreshed expected value", "A C++20 atomic unsigned counter starts at zero with no other changes or wrap. Two threads each prepare expected=0 and desired=1. A's strong CAS succeeds; B's then fails and sets B's expected to 1. B recomputes desired=2 and its next strong CAS succeeds, leaving the joined final counter at 2. Reusing desired=1 after the failure would fail to perform B's intended increment.", ["cas-success", "cas-failure", "expected-refresh", "cas-loop", "lost-update"]),
    ("cpp-spurious-condition-wakeup", "A waiter tests the state after it reacquires the mutex", "A C++20 waiter owns the shared mutex and finds ready=false. Its condition-variable wait releases that mutex, returns spuriously, and reacquires it while ready is still false. A while-loop checks again and waits; an if-only waiter would instead proceed with the false predicate. Every access to ready in this example uses the same mutex.", ["condition-wait", "spurious-wakeup", "predicate-loop", "protected-predicate", "premature-proceed"]),
    ("two-lock-cycle", "Two blocked owners are enough for a closed lock cycle", "In a declared model with single-instance locks M1 and M2, A holds M1 and blocks on M2 while B holds M2 and blocks on M1. Neither releases until obtaining both, and no cancellation, timeout, or external release exists, so both remain deadlocked. Requiring increasing ranks M1<M2 prevents B's M2-then-M1 request. An alternative try-and-release policy removes hold-and-wait but still needs a separate argument against synchronized retry livelock.", ["hold-wait", "wait-cycle", "deadlock", "lock-order", "release-retry"]),
    ("progress-bound-distinction", "Own-step completion does not schedule a stopped process", "In a declared abstract machine with one-step wait-free registers, an operation performs three register reads and one local return step, so it finishes within four of its own steps regardless of other processes' speeds. Pausing that process after step two for a minute delays its return without refuting the own-step property. A merely lock-free competing algorithm can instead keep completing other invocations while this one starves.", ["waitfree-algorithm", "own-steps", "individual-progress", "lockfree-algorithm", "system-progress"]),
    ("aba-pointer-and-tag-cycle", "A two-bit tag only distinguishes histories until it repeats", "In an abstract live-node example, a reader saves head A with tag 0. Transitions A/0 to B/1 to A/2 let an untagged A comparison pass, while an atomic comparison with A/0 fails. With a two-bit wrapping tag, two further transitions B/3 then A/0 restore both components and can fool that old pair comparison too. All node lifetimes stay valid here so the issue is logical history, not dereferencing freed storage.", ["pointer-cas", "aba", "tagged-pointer", "tag-wrap"]),
    ("hazard-revalidation", "A published hazard is retained until the reader's last use", "In a declared SC hazard protocol, R reads source A, publishes A, then revalidates source A before dereferencing. A remover subsequently removes and retires A; its scan sees R's still-published hazard and defers reclamation. R finishes using A and clears the slot. With A still unreachable to new readers and every relevant hazard now absent, the next complete protocol scan may permit reclaiming A.", ["hazard-publish", "hazard-validate", "retired-object", "permitted-reclaim", "reclaimed-use"]),
    ("false-sharing-placement", "Eight-byte fields can share one declared sixty-four-byte line", "For this constructed machine only, coherent lines are 64 bytes and two eight-byte independently owned fields start at actual addresses 0x1000 and 0x1008. CPU 0 repeatedly writes the first while CPU 1 writes the second, so both revisit the line 0x1000 through 0x103f. Placing the second wholly at aligned address 0x1040 removes this pair's false sharing. Throughput improvement still depends on whether the removed coherence traffic had been a bottleneck.", ["cacheline-cohabitation", "false-sharing", "coherence-traffic", "physical-padding", "coherence-stall"]),
